 the two copies receive different inputs, so under A-FDT as interpreted, the other copy's node is a different one from mine. The essay says A-FDT only intervenes on the node for the actual input, leaving the others at their prior values. One remedy is to add edges between logical nodes whose computations are known to coincide, and I'm weighing whether symmetric inputs could justify identifying the two nodes, since the inputs look symmetric in\n\nWorking through the payoffs: if the two nodes are identified, quiet yields 2 and send yields 4, so quiet wins. If they're independent with some prior that the other copy stays quiet, send dominates. Whether I identify them hinges on whether the symmetric inputs justify it. Since the computation is the same and only irrelevant names differ, I'll go with identification and choose quiet.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"This is a twin prisoner's dilemma where the two copies receive different inputs, so under A-FDT as interpreted, the other copy's node is a different one from mine. The essay says A-FDT only intervenes on the node for the actual input, leaving the others at their prior values. One remedy is to add edges between logical nodes whose computations are known to coincide, and I'm weighing whether symmetric inputs could justify identifying the two nodes, since the inputs look symmetric in\n\nWorking through the payoffs: if the two nodes are identified, quiet yields 2 and send yields 4, so quiet wins. If they're independent with some prior that the other copy stays quiet, send dominates. Whether I identify them hinges on whether the symmetric inputs justify it. Since the computation is the same and only irrelevant names differ, I'll go with identification and choose quiet.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9917,"completion_tokens":1152,"total_tokens":110